---
// Case study for paradedb#4763. Queries, rows and plan fragments are quoted
// from the issue (#4567) and the pull request thread; nothing here is re-run.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const QUERY = `SELECT a.ord AS a_ord, b.ord AS b_ord, a.id AS a_id, b.id AS b_id
FROM dup_items a
JOIN dup_items b ON a.grp = b.grp
WHERE a.body @@@ 'left'
  AND b.body @@@ 'right'
ORDER BY a.ord ASC, b.ord ASC
LIMIT 3;`;

const COLUMNS = ["a_ord", "b_ord", "a_id", "b_id"];
// As reported: what JoinScan returned, and what Postgres returns with JoinScan off
const JOINSCAN = [
  ["z100", "a001", "100", "1"],
  ["z200", "a001", "200", "1"],
  ["z300", "a001", "300", "1"],
];
const POSTGRES = [
  ["z100", "a001", "100", "1"],
  ["z100", "a002", "100", "2"],
  ["z100", "a003", "100", "3"],
];

const CAUSE = `active_deferred.dedup_by(|a, b| a.name == b.name);`;

const RESOLVE = `let physical_idx = match matches.len() {
    0 => {
        // Name not found — fall back to the logical index.
        // This should not normally happen; a missing name suggests a
        // plan-construction bug where the SortExec column does not exist
        // in the lookup child schema.
        // …
        logical_idx
    }
    1 => {
        // Unique name — use the exact physical position from the schema.
        matches[0]
    }
    _ => {
        // Duplicate names (self-join) — DataFusion already set col.index()
        // to the correct physical position when building the SortExec.
        // TODO: this relies on a DataFusion invariant that col.index() is
        // correct for the duplicate-name case. It breaks for 3-way self-joins
        // or intermediate Projections that reorder same-name groups.
        // Proper fix: thread explicit column lineage similar to trace_column
        // in late_materialization.rs. Tracked in issue #5093.
        logical_idx
    }
};`;
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Wrong rows from a self-join"
  dek="ParadeDB's JoinScan returned the wrong three rows when a self-join sorted on a column name that both sides share. The cause was one line that compared columns by name. My first fix had the same kind of mistake in it."
  pr={{ ref: "#4763", url: "https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb/pull/4763", merged: "17 May 2026", additions: 274, deletions: 38, files: 5 }}
  issue={{ ref: "#4567", url: "https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb/issues/4567" }}
  next={{ title: "A panic on PG16, and the fix that needed fixing", href: "/work/nested-executor-run" }}
>
  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The query</h2>
    <p>
      The report came with a ten-row table joined to itself. Both sides expose a column called <code>ord</code>, and
      the query sorts by both of them.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={QUERY} lang="sql" theme="css-variables" /></div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The wrong rows</h2>
    <p>With JoinScan on, the query returned three rows. With it off, Postgres returned a different three.</p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-rows">
        <div>
          <p class="cs-rows-title">JoinScan returned</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr>{COLUMNS.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {JOINSCAN.map((row, r) => (
                <tr>{row.map((value, c) => <td class={value !== POSTGRES[r][c] ? "is-off" : undefined}>{value}</td>)}</tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-rows-title">Postgres returns</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr>{COLUMNS.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody class="is-reference">
              {POSTGRES.map((row) => <tr>{row.map((value) => <td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>From the issue. Values in red differ from what Postgres returns for the same query.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Every value JoinScan returned is a real row, and the first row is even right. It is only wrong as an answer to
      this query: the rows are ordered by one of the two sort keys.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The clue</h2>
    <p>
      <code>EXPLAIN</code> showed the top-K node, <code>SegmentedTopKExec</code>, sorting on a single physical column,
      <code>[ord@1 ASC]</code>. The other side's column, <code>ord@3</code>, was gone from the plan.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The cause</h2>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={CAUSE} lang="rust" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <figcaption>late_materialization.rs, before the fix</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      This line removed duplicate deferred columns by name. In a self-join, <code>a.ord</code> and <code>b.ord</code> both
      resolve to the string "ord", so two columns became one. The planner then dropped the second sort key, and the
      lookup node never materialized it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The first fix</h2>
    <p>
      I removed the name-based dedup and rewrote the projection to follow each column by its physical index,
      <code>col.index()</code>, replacing name lookups across <code>late_materialization.rs</code>,
      <code>segmented_topk_rule.rs</code>, <code>tantivy_lookup_exec.rs</code> and <code>pre_filter.rs</code>. The
      ignored test from the report passed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Where my fix was wrong</h2>
    <p>
      During review I had relaxed an assertion that kept failing, on the theory that it was only tripping over
      aliases. A maintainer suspected it was catching something real and asked me to run a second case: two different
      tables that both output a column called <code>name</code>. JoinScan and Postgres disagreed again.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <dl class="cs-panel cs-term cs-kv">
        <div><dt>HashJoinExec projection</dt><dd>[ctid_0@0, name@1, ctid_1@3, name@5]</dd></div>
        <div><dt>the sort wanted</dt><dd class="is-good">p.name, at physical index 3</dd></div>
        <div><dt>SegmentedTopKExec got</dt><dd class="is-bad">col_2@1, which is index 1: s.name</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figcaption>From EXPLAIN VERBOSE on the second case, as I reported it in the thread.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      My rewrite trusted <code>Column.index()</code> from the DataFusion sort node without accounting for how the
      JoinScan projection rearranges the schema. Swapping names for indices had fixed one binding mistake and
      introduced another.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The rebuild</h2>
    <p>
      The merged version resolves each sort column against the child schema, and treats the three possible outcomes
      differently.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={RESOLVE} lang="rust" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <figcaption>From resolve_physical_index in segmented_topk_rule.rs, as merged. "// …" marks lines left out.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Both cases now have regression tests that compare JoinScan with Postgres row by row. The self-join test also
      asserts that both sort keys appear in the plan, so it cannot pass by accident if a later change collapses them
      again:
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term"><span class="is-good">SegmentedTopKExec: expr=[ord@3 ASC NULLS LAST, ord@1 ASC NULLS LAST], k=3</span></div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What it still does not handle</h2>
    <p>
      The duplicate-name branch leans on a DataFusion invariant, not on a real guarantee. A three-way self-join, or a
      projection that reorders columns with the same name, would map to the wrong column silently. That is written
      down in the code as a TODO and tracked as
      <a href="https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb/issues/5093" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">#5093</a>,
      which proposes explicit column lineage in place of the heuristic.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
